{"name":"Rhapsody in Blue","hex":"#002244","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"rhapsody","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An epic poem, or part of one, suitable for uninterrupted recitation.","An exaggeratedly enthusiastic or exalted expression of feeling in speech or writing.\n\n An instrumental composition of irregular form, often incorporating improvisation.\n A random collection or medley; a miscellany; also, a confused string of stories, words, etc.\n\n A literary composition consisting of miscellaneous works."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["Synonym of rhapsodize.\n\nTo say (something) with exaggerated or rapturous enthusiasm.\n To perform (a rhapsody (“an epic poem, or part of one, suitable for uninterrupted recitation”))\n Followed by about, on, over, or upon: to speak with exaggerated or rapturous enthusiasm.","To say (something) with exaggerated or rapturous enthusiasm.\n To perform (a rhapsody (“an epic poem, or part of one, suitable for uninterrupted recitation”))"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/rhapsody"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Rhapsody in Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Rhapsody in Blue is a 1924 musical composition for solo piano and jazz band by George Gershwin. Commissioned by bandleader Paul Whiteman, the work combines elements of classical music with jazz-influenced effects and premiered in a concert titled \"An Experiment in Modern Music\" on February 12, 1924, in Aeolian Hall, New York City. Whiteman's band performed the rhapsody with Gershwin playing the piano. Whiteman's arranger Ferde Grofé orchestrated the rhapsody several times, including the 1924 original scoring, the 1926 pit orchestra scoring, and the 1942 symphonic scoring."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d4/Rhapsody_in_Blue_cover.jpg/330px-Rhapsody_in_Blue_cover.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rhapsody_in_Blue"},{"title":"Rhapsody in Blue (film)","blocks":[{"text":"Rhapsody in Blue, subtitled The story of George Gershwin is a 1945 American biographical film about composer and musician George Gershwin, released by Warner Bros. Pictures. Robert Alda stars as Gershwin. Joan Leslie, Alexis Smith, Hazel Scott, and Anne Brown also star, while Irving Rapper directs. The film premiered in New York on June 27, 1945 and was released in the United States on September 22, 1945."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/6/64/Poster_of_Rhapsody_in_Blue_%28film%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rhapsody_in_Blue_(film)"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/46/Paul_Whiteman_in_Radio_Stars.jpg/500px-Paul_Whiteman_in_Radio_Stars.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/46/Paul_Whiteman_in_Radio_Stars.jpg/1280px-Paul_Whiteman_in_Radio_Stars.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/46/Paul_Whiteman_in_Radio_Stars.jpg/500px-Paul_Whiteman_in_Radio_Stars.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APaul_Whiteman_in_Radio_Stars.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c1/GershwinStandingFacingLeftBain.jpg/500px-GershwinStandingFacingLeftBain.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c1/GershwinStandingFacingLeftBain.jpg/500px-GershwinStandingFacingLeftBain.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGershwinStandingFacingLeftBain.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b1/Experiment_in_Modern_Music_ad_Theatre_Magazine_March_1924.jpg/500px-Experiment_in_Modern_Music_ad_Theatre_Magazine_March_1924.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b1/Experiment_in_Modern_Music_ad_Theatre_Magazine_March_1924.jpg/1280px-Experiment_in_Modern_Music_ad_Theatre_Magazine_March_1924.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b1/Experiment_in_Modern_Music_ad_Theatre_Magazine_March_1924.jpg/500px-Experiment_in_Modern_Music_ad_Theatre_Magazine_March_1924.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Advertisement for An Experiment in Modern Music in the March 1924 issue of Theatre Magazine","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AExperiment_in_Modern_Music_ad_Theatre_Magazine_March_1924.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/06/Aeolian_Hall_%281923%29.jpg/500px-Aeolian_Hall_%281923%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/06/Aeolian_Hall_%281923%29.jpg/500px-Aeolian_Hall_%281923%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Rhapsody premiered on a snowy afternoon at Aeolian Hall, Manhattan, pictured here in 1923.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAeolian_Hall_(1923).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2c/Self-Portrait_of_Carl_Van_Vechten_Crisco_edit.jpg/500px-Self-Portrait_of_Carl_Van_Vechten_Crisco_edit.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2c/Self-Portrait_of_Carl_Van_Vechten_Crisco_edit.jpg/1280px-Self-Portrait_of_Carl_Van_Vechten_Crisco_edit.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2c/Self-Portrait_of_Carl_Van_Vechten_Crisco_edit.jpg/500px-Self-Portrait_of_Carl_Van_Vechten_Crisco_edit.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASelf-Portrait_of_Carl_Van_Vechten_Crisco_edit.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/05/Marguerite_d%27Alvarez_001.png/330px-Marguerite_d%27Alvarez_001.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/05/Marguerite_d%27Alvarez_001